# Kubernetes Workloads
Workloads are Kubernetes objects used to run and manage applications inside a cluster.

A workload tells Kubernetes:
What application to run
How many Pods to create
How to update Pods
How to recover from failures

> Types of Kubernetes Workloads
Workload	  Purpose
Pod	        Run containers
ReplicaSet	Maintain Pod count
Deployment	Manage application lifecycle
StatefulSet	Stateful applications
DaemonSet	  One Pod per node
Job	        Run task once
CronJob	    Run task on schedule

1. Namespaces
A Namespace is used to logically divide and organize resources within a Kubernetes cluster.
> Why Use Namespaces?
Resource isolation
Environment separation (Dev, Test, Prod)
Better resource management
Example
Cluster
 ├── Dev Namespace
 ├── Test Namespace
 └── Prod Namespace

2. Pods
A Pod is the smallest Kubernetes object that runs one or more containers.
> Features
Contains one or more containers
Shared network and storage
Runs application workloads
Example
Pod
 └── Nginx Container

3. Deployments
Deployment is used to deploy, update, and manage application Pods in Kubernetes.
> Features
Scaling
Rolling Updates
Rollback
Self-Healing
> Example
Deployment
    ↓
ReplicaSet
    ↓
Pods

4. ReplicaSets
ReplicaSet maintains a fixed number of identical Pods running at all times.
> Example
Desired Pods = 3
Pod-1
Pod-2
Pod-3
If one Pod fails, ReplicaSet automatically creates a new Pod.

5. static pod
5. manual scheduling

5. Labels & Selectors
Labels identify Kubernetes resources, while Selectors use those labels to find and connect resources.
> Labels
Labels are key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects.
> Example:
labels:
  app: nginx
  env: prod

>Selectors
Selectors find and connect resources using labels.
> Example:
selector:
  app: nginx

6. Rolling Updates
Rolling Update replaces old Pods with new Pods gradually without affecting application availability.
> Process
Old Pod → New Pod
Old Pod → New Pod
Old Pod → New Pod
> Benefits
Zero downtime
Safer deployments
Continuous availability

7. RollbackRollback reverts an application deployment to its previous working version.
> Example
Version 1 → Version 2 ❌
Rollback
Version 2 → Version 1 ✅

8. DaemonSets
A DaemonSet is a Kubernetes object that ensures a copy of a Pod runs on every node or on selected nodes in a cluster.

When a new node is added to the cluster, Kubernetes automatically creates the DaemonSet Pod on that node. When a node is removed, its Pod is also removed.
> Use Cases
Monitoring
Logging
Security agents
> Example
Node-1 → Pod
Node-2 → Pod
Node-3 → Pod

9. Jobs
A Job is used when you want a Pod to perform a task and finish successfully.

Unlike a Deployment, which keeps applications running continuously, a Job is designed for one-time or finite tasks.
> Use Cases
Database migration
Backup
Data processing
> Flow
Start
  ↓
Task
  ↓
Complete

10. CronJobs
A CronJob is used to run a Job automatically according to a schedule.
It is similar to Linux cron.
> Examples
Daily Backup
Weekly Reports
Log Cleanup
Flow
12:00 AM
   ↓
  Job
   ↓
Complete



